# Role wrappers vs refs

A composite (`Order`, `Money`, …) does **not** take `Instrument` or `InstrumentRef` as a field type. It takes a **role wrapper**: a thin generic newtype named for the field's role (`OrderInstrument<OI>`, `OrderSize<OS>`, `MoneyCurrency<MC>`).

The wrapper's parameter is the payload. That payload is **either**:

1. A **concrete** domain type (`Instrument<IID>`, `Quantity<QTY>`, `Currency<CID, CC>`, …), when the value is fully known.
2. A **`*Ref` enum** from `crates/lull-spec/src/enums/refs/`, when the value is identified one of several ways.

`*Ref` enums already collapse those ways (`InstrumentRef::Id`, `CurrencyRef::Id` / `Code`, `OrderSizeRef::Quantity` / `Lots`, …). The wrapper stays the same either way; only the type argument changes.

```rust
// BAD — Order is coupled to one representation of "instrument"
pub struct Order<IID> {
    pub instrument: Instrument<IID>,
}

// BAD — Order is coupled to the ref enum; a concrete Instrument cannot be the field
pub struct Order<IID> {
    pub instrument: InstrumentRef<IID>,
}

// GOOD — role wrapper; payload is Instrument or InstrumentRef (or another inner type)
pub struct OrderInstrument<OI>(OI);
pub struct Order<OID, OI> {
    pub instrument: OrderInstrument<OI>,
}

// call sites
OrderInstrument::new(instrument);
OrderInstrument::new(InstrumentRef::Id(id));
```

- Put new `*Ref` enums under `src/enums/refs/`, not next to `OrderSide` / `OrderType`.
- Do not skip the wrapper to "save" a type parameter: the wrapper is the stable field type.
- Keep the wrapper's generic mnemonic from the wrapper name (`OI`, `OS`, `MC`); inner `*Ref` / newtypes keep their own (`IID`, `QTY`, …).
